[StudyName "Jobava London Naroditsky style"]
[ChapterName "Jobava London - Idea #6: e3-e4 Pawn Break"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6JdHL1yF/oR4N6Rph"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/5ppp/p3pn2/1ppp4/3P1B2/P1N1PN2/1PP2PPP/R2QKB1R w KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Orientation "white"]

{ Throughout the course, you will notice that White operates effectively on all three sides of the board: queenside, center, and kingside. Typical idea #1 involves using the center efficiently by maneuvering the knight to e5. But another very common idea involves engineering the central breakthrough e3-e4. Of course, we see the move e4 played in response to ...c5, but in most other lines - such as the 3...a6 variation - we prepare this break gradually, by deploying our bishop to d3 and queen to e2. First, we must end the central tension to prevent Black from pushing away our bishop with ...c4. } 1. dxc5 Bxc5 2. Bd3 { We are ready to push e3-e4, but Black does not have to cooperate. } 2... Bb7 3. Qe2 { Very important. All ingredients are now in place for e4, but we do not have to rush - generally, it is a good idea to castle first so that our king is not caught in the crossfire once the center opens. Following that, we will be ready to push e4, opening the center and enabling us to more effectively take advantage of our superior piece activity. Once the d-file opens, the Black queen can also be pushed around with Rad1. } { [%cal Re3e4] } *


[Event "Jobava London Naroditsky style: Jobava London - Idea #7: Knight Jump to b5"]
[Date "2023.01.29"]
[White "Jobava London"]
[Black "Idea #7: Knight Jump to b5"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "BortnykChess"]
[FEN "rnbqk2r/pp2ppbp/5np1/2pp4/3P1B1P/2N1P3/PPP2PP1/R2QKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Jobava London Naroditsky style"]
[ChapterName "Jobava London - Idea #7: Knight Jump to b5"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6JdHL1yF/ZJkFmc6C"]
[FEN "rnbqk2r/pp2ppbp/5np1/2pp4/3P1B1P/2N1P3/PPP2PP1/R2QKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Orientation "white"]

{ On move two of the Jobava London, we develop the knight to c3, violating the most basic principle of Queen's Pawn openings. Typical #1 showed one way in which we can compensate for that: by quickly activating our other knight. However, the queen's knight is far from a lost cause: on c3, it controls the crucial e4-square. But one of the most ubiquitous ideas in the Jobava London involves hopping to b5, threatening Nc7 and forcing Black to seriously compromise the placement of his own queen's knight. In this position, which obviously stems from the 3...g6 variation, Black has just carried out the standard ...c7-c5 pawn break. This affords us a perfect opportunity to initiate the knight jump: } 1. Nb5 { It may seem rather strange that a move which creates an ultra-prosaic threat is effective, but you should remember that we are not banking on Black missing Nc7 (although, in certain cases, such as the 3...Nc6 line, Black just actually does run into a situation where there is no defense against Nc7!). Rather, the point of this move is entirely positional. } 1... Na6 2. c3 { We frequently follow up by solidifying the d4-pawn, London-style. In this position, the power of the knight jump is on full display: Black has no easy way to kick the knight out, and it can be further supported with a3-a4. In lines where Black's pawn is on c7 (particularly 3...e6), Black can force our knight out with ...c6, but this does not make Nb5 any less efficient, since Black will still have a hard time finding suitable prospects for his knight. The knight jump to b5 is yet another illustration of the multifaceted nature of the Jobava London, which features countless ideas that are both positional and tactical. } *


[Event "Jobava London Naroditsky style: Jobava London - Idea #8: Dealing with ...Bb4 and ...Ne4"]
[Date "2023.01.29"]
[White "Jobava London"]
[Black "Idea #8: Dealing with ...Bb4 and ...Ne4"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "BortnykChess"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/p4ppp/p1p1pn2/3p4/1b1P1B2/2N1P3/PPP2PPP/R2QK1NR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Jobava London Naroditsky style"]
[ChapterName "Jobava London - Idea #8: Dealing with ...Bb4 and ...Ne4"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6JdHL1yF/HVy1uE2T"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/p4ppp/p1p1pn2/3p4/1b1P1B2/2N1P3/PPP2PPP/R2QK1NR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Orientation "white"]

{ Black has a wide choice when it comes to the deployment of his dark-squared bishop. Most of the time, it is developed to either e7 or d6, but in some variations (such as in the line 3...e6 4.Nb5 Na6 5.e3 c6 6.Nc3, which is the one in front of you), Black can also develop the bishop more aggressively to b4. The instinctual reaction here is a2-a3, but it turns out that the so-called "threat" - to pressure our knight with ...Ne4 - requires absolutely no action on our part. As long as we can castle in response to ...Ne4 (to avoid losing an exchange), we are more than happy to part ways with a pawn in return for a tremendous initiative and total control over the queenside. } 1. Nf3 Ne4 2. O-O! { The key. } 2... Nxc3 { Black can also take with the bishop first, but that changes very little (see analysis). } 3. bxc3 Bxc3 4. Rb1 { It is clear, even to an untrained eye, that White's compensation is nothing short of massive. The bishop on c3 is vulnerable, the b-file is ours, the other bishop has no prospects, and we can pile on the pressure in several obvious ways, such as Qd3 and Ne5. Some of these things are unique to this position, but in almost all variations, Black faces tremendous difficulties upon capturing the pawn on e3. Don't be afraid to sacrifice this pawn! } *


[Event "Jobava London Naroditsky style: Jobava London - 3...c6"]
[Date "2023.01.29"]
[White "Jobava London"]
[Black "3...c6"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "BortnykChess"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "D01"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[StudyName "Jobava London Naroditsky style"]
[ChapterName "Jobava London - 3...c6"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6JdHL1yF/7Yil631T"]
[Orientation "white"]

{ [%evp 0,33,28,29,27,18,26,26,52,32,84,74,62,43,42,43,32,27,27,27,27,17,31,-3,0,2,55,40,43,71,77,82,78,46,59,70] } 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Bf4 c6 { This move does not have too much independent value. Most of the time, Black follows up with 4...Bf5, transposing to a line that we analyze via the 3...Bf5 move order (3...Bf5 4.Nf3 c6). In this file, we give the one legitimate independent variation, in which Black quickly develops their queen to b6 and probes for queenside weaknesses. } 4. e3 Qb6 (4... Bf5 { See 3...Bf5 4.Nf3 c6. }) 5. Rb1 { Not the prettiest looking move, but it gets the job done. Furthermore, we never castle queenside in this line, so there is no chance that we will regret this move down the line. Finally, the rook on b1 can be a major asset in the ensuing queenside battle, helping to prepare the typical b4-b5 minority attack. } (5. a3!? { This is not our main recommendation, but it is a cute trap nonetheless: } 5... Qxb2?? 6. Na4 $18) 5... Bf5 6. Bd3 { We develop the bishop and immediately confront Black with an important decision. } 6... Bxd3 (6... e6 7. Bxf5 exf5 { This exchange may seem to favor Black due to his increased control over the e4-square, but in reality, the f5-pawn is a serious target that we now use as fuel for a furious kingside expansion. } 8. h3 { White's intentions are far more violent than simply making luft for the bishop on h2. We are preparing to tear Black apart with g2-g4! } 8... Be7 (8... h5 { This stops g2-g4, but creates more holes on the kingside. We now transition to straightforward development, and follow up with pawn play on the queenside. } 9. Nf3 Nbd7 10. O-O Ne4 (10... Be7 11. Ne2 { A versatile move, with two main ideas. The knight can be repurposed to d3 (via f4, once the bishop moves), and by un-blocking the c-pawn, we are preparing rapid queenside expansion with an eventual c2-c4. } 11... Ne4 (11... O-O 12. b3 { Everything is ready for the queenside attack! } 12... Qa6 13. c4! { Anyway! } 13... dxc4 (13... Qxa2 14. Ra1 Qb2 15. Qd3 dxc4 16. bxc4 { In order to un-trap the queen, Black has to give up f5: } 16... Qb6 17. Rfb1 Qd8 18. Qxf5 $18 { Note that we avoided 18.Rxb7 because of Nc5! White's massive queenside pressure, coupled with our pawn superiority in the center, grants us a decisive advantage. }) 14. bxc4 Qxc4 15. Rxb7 $14 { Black's queenside is in tatters, and he suffers from weaknesses on the other side of the board as well. }) 12. Nd2 { Getting rid of Black's strongest piece. } 12... g5 { An attempt to jump-start a kingside attack, but it falls rather short. } (12... Ndf6 13. Nxe4 fxe4 (13... Nxe4 14. f3 { Pushing away the knight. } 14... Nf6 15. Qd3 { Attacking f5 and inducing yet another weakness. } 15... g6 16. Be5 $16 { White's next step is to blast open the center with e3-e4. } { [%cal Re3e4] }) 14. c4 { Time for the queenside pawn storm! This positional sacrifice shatters Black's central pawn chain. } 14... dxc4 (14... O-O 15. Be5 { The knight is coming to f4, and Black is forced to capture on c4 anyway. } { [%cal Re2f4] } 15... dxc4 { See 14..dxc4 15.Be5 0-0. }) 15. Be5 { First, we centralize the bishop. The ability to take on f6 will serve us well in the long run, especially if our knight moves on to c3 or g3. } 15... O-O 16. b3! { Prying open the b-file. } 16... cxb3 17. Rxb3 Qa6 18. Ng3 $16 { White's compensation for the pawn is overwhelming: we are already threatening to win the pawn back with Nxh5 and Bxf6-Nxe4, but the prospect of Nf5 is even scarier. } { [%cal Rg3h5,Rg3f5] }) 13. Bh2 g4 14. Kh1 { Calmly withdrawing the king to a safer spot, underscoring the ineffectiveness of Black's pawn invasion. Black's monarch, on the other hand, has no safe haven. Our next move is c2-c4, prying open the queenside and leading to the collapse of Black's pawn structure. A sample line: } { [%cal Rc2c4] } 14... O-O-O 15. c4 Nxd2 16. Qxd2 dxc4 17. b3 { The same idea that we saw in the 12...Ndf6 13.Nxe4 fxe4 14.c4 line, forcing open the b-file and leading to a long-lasting attack against Black's king. } 17... cxb3 18. Rxb3 Qa6 19. Qc2 $18 { Targeting f5 and x-raying Black's king. Rfb1 is coming next. } { [%cal Rf1b1,Rc2f5] }) (10... g6 11. Ne2 Bg7 12. b3 O-O 13. c4 $16) 11. Nxe4 fxe4 12. Nd2 Be7 13. c4 { Undermining Black's pawn chain, and starting a furious queenside pawn storm. } 13... Nf6 14. b4! { Simple and powerful. We threaten to blast open the queenside with b4-b5, and the pawn is untouchable due to the pin against the queen. } 14... a6 15. a4 $14 { White is having all the fun: apart from the imminent threat of b4-b5, there are many ways to improve the position and accentuate the pressure, such as Qb3 and Be5. If Black is forced to take on c4, the e4-pawn will become a long-term weakness. Also note the awkwardness of the h5-pawn: anytime Black castles, Be5 will carry the additional threat of Bxf6 and Qxh5. }) (8... Bb4 9. g4 { Ignoring the possibility of ...Ne4, which doesn't bother us! } 9... Ne4 (9... fxg4 10. hxg4 Ne4 11. a3 Nxc3 (11... Ba5 12. Ne2 $16) 12. bxc3 Bxc3+ { Now, time for some Jobava London magic: the intuitive move is 13.Kf1, but we use the king as an agent of attack: } 13. Ke2! Qa6+ 14. Qd3! Qxd3+ 15. Kxd3 { The idea is revealed: the bishop on c3 is under attack, and Black has no time to defend b7. His position collapses. } 15... Ba5 16. Rxb7 $18) 10. f3! { The genius of the rook's placement on b1 is revealed in this line: by sacrificing the c3-pawn, we open up the rook and reveal the hidden vulnerability of Black's queen. Black cannot hang on to the extra pawn, since f5 hangs as well. } 10... Nxc3 11. bxc3 Bxc3+ 12. Kf2 Qa6 13. gxf5 Nd7 14. Qe2 Qxe2+ (14... Qxa2 15. Rxb7 $18) 15. Nxe2 $18 { [%cal Re2c3,Rb1b7] }) 9. g4 fxg4 10. hxg4 Nbd7 11. g5 Ne4 12. Nxe4 dxe4 13. c3 O-O-O 14. b4 $16 { White's queenside attack is virtually unstoppable. } { [%cal Gc3c4,Gb4b5] }) (6... Nbd7?? { An incredibly common blunder! } 7. Bxf5 $18) (6... Bg6 { With this move, Black keeps the tension and tosses the ball back into White's court. } 7. h4 { A typical Jobava move: we aim to force Black to trade bishops on our terms. By forcing Black to take on d3, we will create the desirable d3-d4-e3 pawn mass, enabling us to assert our dominance in the center and on the queenside. } 7... e6 (7... h5? { As usual, this move loosens Black's control over the g6-square, making the trade far more desirable! } 8. Bxg6 fxg6 9. Qd3 Kf7 10. Nf3 $18 { [%cal Rf3e5,Rf3g5] }) 8. h5 Bxd3 9. cxd3 { The desirable structure has been obtained. Now, it is time to develop our kingside and expand in the center. } 9... h6 10. Nf3 Be7 (10... Nbd7 11. e4 Bb4 12. e5 { The knight has nowhere good to go, since d7 is taken up by the other knight! } 12... Ng4 13. a3 { Note the power of the rook on b1, which shines through in the event that Black trades on c3. The awkwardness of Black's queen is on full display here. } 13... Be7 14. Na4 Qa6 15. Ng1! f5 (15... f6 16. Qxg4 Qxa4 17. Qxg7 Rf8 18. Bxh6 $18) 16. f3 $18 { It turns out that the knight on g4 is trapped! }) 11. e4 O-O 12. e5 { Driving the pawn down and gaining more space. } 12... Nfd7 13. Rh3 $16 { A sexy move indeed! We lift the rook up to g3, creating massive threats against Black's king, which has just lost its last loyal defender. White's pawn structure is visually unappealing, but our central pawn mass does a fantastic job of holding Black's counterplay at bay. For a more thorough explanation, see the note to Black's 11th move in the main line (6...Bxd3 7.cxd3). } { [%cal Rh3g3,Re1f1,Rf1g1] } 13... c5 14. dxc5 Nxc5 { Now, it is important to avoid 15.d4, which grants Black the e4-square. Instead, we use our other pawn to push Black's knight back. } 15. b4 Ncd7 16. Kf1 { A little prophylaxis before launching the kingside attack. We've taken the sting out of ...Bxb4, since the knight will no longer hang with check. } 16... Bxb4 (16... Nc6 17. b5 Na5 18. Qd2 Kh7 19. g4 $40 { The attack commences! Black has nothing going on the Q-side, while we are fully ready to tear Black apart on the other side of the board. }) 17. a3 $18) 7. cxd3 { As usual, we recapture with the pawn (see typical ideas #1 for a more thorough explanation of the potency of White's central pawn mass). We now carry out a typical set of steps: first, we complete our kingside development. Following that, we initiate central expansion with e3-e4, giving us a pleasant and easy game. } 7... e6 8. Nf3 Be7 9. O-O { Step 1 has been achieved. } 9... O-O 10. e4 { Time for phase 2, expanding in the center and preparing to shove the pawn down to e5, cramping Black even further. } (10. b4!? { A worthwhile alternative, initiating immediate queenside play along typical lines. Of course, the pawn is untouchable due to a3. } 10... a6 11. a4 Bxb4 (11... Nbd7 12. a5 Qd8 13. h4 $14) 12. Bd6! $16 { A bolt from the blue, winning at least an exchange. }) 10... Nbd7 11. e5 Ne8 { It is worth pausing and appreciating the unique potency of White's central pawn structure. The d4-e5/e6-d5 pawn chains resemble an Advanced French, with the chief difference being the additional presence of White's pawn on d3. Visually, White's pawn mass appears rather ugly, but the pawn on d3 is actually a great asset. Any time Black pushes the c-pawn - and this is Black's only active idea - White can capture on c5 and rebuild the pawn chain with d3-d4, highlighting the hidden value inherent in having doubled pawns! } 12. h4 { Expanding on the kingside. Just because we are castled kingside certainly does not mean that we can't attack on that side of the board! Note that this move does not commit us to anything in particular: the pawn on h4 is not a serious weakness, and we are not obliged to follow up with h4-h5. We are in charge on all three sides of the board: on ther queenside, we are ready to act with b2-b4. In the center, Black has nothing going and 12...c5 fails to break up our center (see main line). On the kingside, we can expand gradually with h5, g2-g3, Kg2, Rh1, etc. } 12... c5 { Black aims to generate a semblance of activity. } (12... h6 13. Ne2 { White aims to regroup the minor pieces by retreating the bishop to e3 (this also discourages ...c5), and repositioning the knight on f4, from where it can jump to h5. } 13... c5 14. dxc5 Nxc5 15. Be3 Qa6 16. b4!? { Using tactics to force the knight out of c5. Once again, it is important to refrain from 16.d4, which would grant Black a powerful post on e4. } 16... Nxd3 (16... Nd7 17. a4 Rc8 18. Qb3 $16 { Black is pushed to the edge of the board, and we will wrest the c-file from his control with Rfc1 on the next move. }) 17. b5 Qa3 18. Rb3 $18) (12... f6 { This idea is typical for French Advance-type positions, but in this case it falls short. } 13. g3 { Reinforcing the bishop and taking the sting out of ...fxe5. } 13... fxe5 14. dxe5 c5 15. d4 { Meeting Black at the top! This move grants Black a protected passer in the center, but enables us to establish complete dominance over that pawn by gaining the d4-square for our knight. } 15... cxd4 16. Nxd4 { Black lacks the piece activity and coordination to kick the knight out of d4. Furthermore, the e6-pawn is a long-term weakness that Black must babysit at all times. } 16... Nc7 (16... Bxh4 17. b4! { Ignoring the situation on the kingside! In reality, we are preparing a devious maneuver that will force Black to lose contact with the e6-pawn. } 17... Be7 18. Na4 Qa6 19. b5 Qa5 20. Nxe6 $18) 17. Rc1 { Simple chess! Of course, we could not care less about the b2-pawn, for many reasons (such as 17...Qxb2 18.Na4, picking off the c7-knight). } 17... Rac8 18. h5 { Playing on both flanks! We expand on the kingside, preparing a timely h5-h6. } 18... Qxb2 19. Qg4 { Now, the queen jumps into the game! } 19... Nc5 20. Ncb5! { Deflecting the c7-knight from its control over e6. } 20... Nxb5 21. Nxe6 Rf7 (21... Nxe6 22. Qxe6+ Kh8 23. Rxc8 $18) 22. Nxg7! { Classic Jobava London action! The rook on c8 hangs! } 22... Rc6 23. Nf5+ Kh8 24. Rb1 Qc2 25. Nxe7 Rxe7 26. Rxb5 $18 { Gaining back the piece, with a clearly overwhelming position. }) 13. Na4 { An important move, which ensures that we will remove the knight from d7 as a result of the trade on c5. This will leave Black with a far more passive knight on e8, and grant us the ability to pressure the queenside without obstruction. } 13... Qb5 14. Nxc5 Nxc5 15. dxc5 Bxc5 16. Rc1 Be7 17. Qd2 $16 { Defending b2 and coordinating the rooks. We have countless ways to improve our position, such as d3-d4, Rc2-Rfc1, as well as h4-h5. Black has no easy way to improve the placement of the knight, and suffers from a general lack of coordination and lack of space. } *


